<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gilgit Agency</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1877–1947</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgit">Gilgit</a> Agency was a British political agency in the far north of the Indian empire, set up in 1877 and permanently from 1889, to watch the passes towards Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. It supervised the states of Hunza and Nagar and the surrounding valleys under the suzerainty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>. Leased to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1935, it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> after a local revolt in 1947.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gilgit-agency.jpg" alt="Gilgit Agency" width="320" height="249" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National Geographic · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jammu_and_Kashmir_in_1946_map_of_India_by_National_Geographic.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
